<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Chobanids of Kastamonu</h1><p class="st-meta">1211 CE – 1309 CE (approx.) · capital Kastamonu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Turkish frontier dynasty that ruled Kastamonu for the Seljuks and led their naval expedition across the Black Sea to the Crimea.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husam-al-din-choban">Husam al-Din Choban</a> was a commander of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum</a> who was given the frontier district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kastamonu">Kastamonu</a>, facing Byzantine and later Komnenian lands to the north, in the early 13th century. His family held it as a hereditary march.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Around 1220-1221 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husam-al-din-choban">Choban</a> led a Seljuk expedition by sea from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sinope">Sinop</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudak">Sudak</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>, an event recorded by the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-bibi">Ibn Bibi</a>. His successors ruled as marcher lords, leading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> warriors in raids on Byzantine territory. Under Muzaffar al-Din Yavlak Arslan the family acted increasingly independently as Seljuk power declined under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1309 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suleyman-pasha-candarid">Suleyman Pasha</a>, from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/candarids">Candarids</a>, killed the last Chobanid ruler Mahmud and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kastamonu">Kastamonu</a>, which became the Candarid capital.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="emirate, 1211 CE – 1309 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">emirate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Anatolia and the Levant</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:83.80%;width:1.95%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1211 CE – 1309 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kastamonu</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Husam al-Din Choban, Muzaffar al-Din Yavlak Arslan, Mahmud</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Turkish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Çobanoğulları</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1260&amp;focus=chobanids-of-kastamonu" aria-label="Chobanids of Kastamonu on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5504 702 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Chobanids of Kastamonu lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5824 958L5824 958M5824 958L5810 1072"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5824 958h0M5810 1072h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5824 958h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5824 958h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kastamonu. <a href="/atlas?year=1260&amp;focus=chobanids-of-kastamonu">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
